// Original lessons: compare expected controls with evidence, then verify repairs.
const h=v=>({t:'h',v}),p=v=>({t:'p',v}),key=v=>({t:'key',v}),tip=v=>({t:'tip',v});
const term=(v,note)=>({t:'new',v,note}),check=(q,a,why)=>({q,a,why}),ref=(title,url)=>({title,url});
module.exports={
 '6.1':{
  objective:'Explain an approved security baseline, build a small fictional software inventory, distinguish configuration drift from an attack, and describe a tested update lifecycle.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Begin with what the system needs to do'),
   p('Week 5 followed an application request through required checks. Week 6 asks whether the systems supporting that application remain maintained and appropriately configured. Maya’s fictional class laptop must open lessons, save notes and receive approved updates. A setting that blocks every task is not automatically a good security decision. Start with the service’s purpose, the data it holds and the people responsible for it.'),
   term('Security baseline','An approved set of expected settings and controls for a particular system role. It should have an owner, a version, a scope and a review date. A baseline for a classroom laptop need not be identical to one for a public web server.'),
   term('Hardening and configuration drift','Hardening reduces unnecessary exposure or privileges through appropriate configuration. Drift is a difference between observed settings and the approved baseline. Drift can result from maintenance, mistakes or authorised exceptions; it is not automatically evidence of an attack.'),
   p('Fictional baseline: ordinary study uses a standard account; supported software has approved updates; the host firewall is enabled under the appropriate policy; unnecessary sharing is disabled; a separate recovery copy exists. For each item, write what observation would verify it. Do not copy every setting from an unrelated checklist onto your real device. Compatibility, ownership and a recovery route matter.'),
   h('2. Know what is installed and who maintains it'),
   term('Software inventory and support lifecycle','An inventory records assets/software with identifiers, installed versions, source, owner and relevant support information. A support lifecycle indicates when a vendor or distributor provides maintenance under a stated policy. An unfamiliar version string alone does not establish support status.'),
   p('Example inventory row: asset LAB-LAPTOP-A; application FictionalReader; version 2.4; source approved school package; owner lab administrator; observation from the inventory report; observed time recorded. A second row has an unknown version. Keep it unknown rather than copying the desired version into the observed field. An inventory that only lists approved software cannot reveal an unexpected installation unless it also captures the actual system within the permitted scope.'),
   p('Versions can include build numbers, distribution revisions and backported fixes. Do not compare them as simple alphabetical strings and infer security. A distributor may deliver a fix without matching an upstream product’s visible version exactly. Consult the applicable vendor/distributor advisory and package information. This lesson uses invented products; their version numbers do not represent real advisories.'),
   h('3. Updating is a lifecycle, not a green button'),
   term('Patch, update and verification','A patch changes software to address an issue; an update can include security or other changes. Verification checks that the intended update took effect and that necessary functions still work. Downloaded, installed and active after restart can be different states.'),
   p('A reasonable lifecycle is: identify affected assets → use trusted update information/source → choose priority and an approved change plan → preserve the required recovery option → apply the change → complete required restart/redeployment → verify the active state and important functions → record evidence. A message saying Download complete does not establish that the vulnerable running component was replaced.'),
   term('Change window and rollback plan','A change window is the agreed period for maintenance. A rollback plan explains how to recover from a failed change without assuming that restoring an older vulnerable version is a permanent fix. Important emergency work may need a faster authorised route than routine scheduling.'),
   p('Worked example: FictionalReader needs an approved update. Maya records its current state, asks the responsible lab owner to plan the change, and tests opening a fictional worksheet afterward. If a restart is required but pending, the finding is not marked verified fixed. If the update breaks the learning task, the owner uses the agreed recovery process and records the remaining security exposure while arranging a suitable solution.'),
   h('4. Compare three evidence states'),
   p('A baseline comparison should distinguish match, mismatch and unknown. Match: the approved setting was observed as expected. Mismatch: the observed setting differs. Unknown: the permitted evidence was unavailable or too old. Missing evidence is not a pass, but it is also not automatically a confirmed violation. Document what must be checked next and who can do it.'),
   p('If a deliberate exception is needed, record its reason, scope, approver, temporary controls and review/expiry condition. An approved exception is not the same as an unrecorded discrepancy, and it does not remove the underlying risk. A baseline itself also needs review when roles, dependencies or vendor guidance change.'),
   h('5. Where Python helps without changing settings'),
   p('Python can later compare approved inventory/configuration records with a known baseline and produce match/mismatch/unknown rows. It should retain asset identity, observation time and source. It should not invent a missing version, silently apply fixes or equate a text difference with exploitation. Today’s exercise is to design that comparison on paper using fictional records.'),
   key('Define a role-specific baseline, preserve observed facts and verify the active result of an authorised change. An update announcement or a drift flag alone is not the final security conclusion.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('An observed setting differs from the baseline. Does that prove an attacker changed it?','No. It establishes a discrepancy requiring context and investigation.','Approved changes, mistakes and exceptions can also produce drift. Keep observation and cause separate.'),
   check('The update downloaded, but a required restart is still pending. Is the active fix verified?','Not from that evidence. Check the installed/running state after the required maintenance steps.','Downloading an update and activating its fix are different parts of the lifecycle.'),
   check('The inventory report cannot obtain a software version. Which comparison state fits?','Unknown, with a documented follow-up and evidence limitation.','Do not treat absent evidence as either a known match or a confirmed affected version.'),
   check('Why should a baseline identify its role and owner?','Its controls must fit the intended work, and someone must approve, maintain and review them.','A checklist from another system may break legitimate tasks or omit the relevant responsibilities.')
  ],
  reflection:'Write a five-item fictional laptop baseline and three inventory rows. Give each comparison a match/mismatch/unknown result with evidence. Then outline an update plan, verification step and recovery dependency without changing your real device.',
  references:[ref('NIST: patch-management lifecycle','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/40/r4/final'),ref('NIST: cybersecurity risk and control context','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework')]
 },
 '6.2':{
  objective:'Separate vulnerability identity, severity and local risk; assess applicability/exposure using evidence; and prioritise fictional findings without interpreting a score as proof of compromise.',
  sections:[
   h('1. A finding begins with a claim to verify'),
   term('Vulnerability and affected condition','A vulnerability is a weakness that can undermine a security requirement under relevant conditions. Its applicability can depend on the product, version, enabled feature, configuration and other prerequisites. A scanner label is a starting observation, not automatic proof of the whole condition.'),
   p('Maya receives an approved fictional report about a note service. It says a software component may be affected. Before choosing a repair, identify the exact asset, product/build, source of the claim and relevant advisory. A product name occurring in an inventory is weaker evidence than a verified affected component and configuration. Do not test exploitation against a real service to settle a beginner worksheet.'),
   term('CVE and advisory','A CVE identifier provides a common reference for a publicly recorded vulnerability. It is an identifier, not an automatic score or a complete remediation plan. An advisory supplies details such as affected conditions and supported fixes; check its source and revision.'),
   p('Do not assume every local misconfiguration has a CVE, or that absence of a CVE means no security concern. Public access to a private-data folder can violate a policy even without a software defect identifier. Conversely, a CVE for a product does not automatically apply to every deployment of that product. This course uses training labels such as TRAIN-A rather than invented real-looking CVE records.'),
   h('2. Severity is one input, not the priority decision'),
   term('CVSS, vector and score','CVSS is a framework for describing vulnerability severity. A vector records metric choices; a numeric score alone omits that context. Different versions and metric groups should be identified. Base severity does not by itself describe your organisation’s complete risk or probability of attack.'),
   term('Local risk and confidence','Local risk considers relevant likelihood and consequences for the actual asset and controls. Confidence describes how well evidence supports your finding. High severity with uncertain applicability and confirmed harmful exposure are different report situations.'),
   p('A score of 9.8 is not a 98 percent probability of attack. A high score does not prove exploitation occurred, and a low score does not prove an acceptable business consequence. Keep source severity and your local priority in separate fields. Include the reason: affected service role, exposure, known exploitation information, sensitive data, available controls and feasible repair.'),
   h('3. Exposure and exploitation describe different facts'),
   term('Exposure, reachability and exploitability','Exposure concerns accessible surfaces or data. Reachability concerns whether a relevant path can reach the component from a stated location. Exploitability concerns whether a weakness can be used under its necessary conditions. None of these alone is proof that someone already exploited it.'),
   p('A deliberately public lesson page is exposed by design and may have no particular identified exploitable flaw. A vulnerable component behind an access boundary still matters because authorised access can be misused or another boundary can fail. State from where a service is reachable and what controls were observed. Do not convert not internet-facing into no risk, or an open service into confirmed compromise.'),
   term('Known exploitation and KEV','Known exploitation information can influence urgency. CISA’s Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog records vulnerabilities with evidence of exploitation in the wild. It is an input to prioritisation, not a record that your own asset was compromised. Absence from a catalog is not proof that exploitation is impossible.'),
   h('4. Work through a context-first comparison'),
   p('Fictional TRAIN-A: a confirmed affected public note service, reachable from the internet, with sensitive records and a supported fix available. Fictional TRAIN-B: an old component name appears in an offline test image, but the active version and feature status are unknown. TRAIN-A needs prompt owner-led containment/repair and verification. TRAIN-B needs targeted evidence collection rather than being ignored or claimed confirmed fixed. A real priority also follows the responsible organisation’s policy and current advisory information.'),
   p('A temporary access restriction can reduce one route while a fix is prepared; it does not prove the underlying weakness disappeared. Record what was restricted, from which source, the owner, duration and a follow-up test. Evidence of an actual incident triggers the incident-response route, not only a patch ticket. Week 7 will expand that distinction.'),
   h('5. Write a defensible priority row'),
   p('Use fields: training finding ID, asset, source/advisory, applicability evidence, reachability context, potential consequence, existing control, confidence, priority reason, owner and next action. Do not replace every unknown with zero or calculate a homemade score that pretends to be official CVSS. A clear reasoned priority is more useful than an unexplained large number.'),
   key('Identify the actual affected condition, keep severity separate from local priority, and state what exposure and evidence really establish. Prioritise with context and revisit uncertain findings.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Is CVSS 9.8 a 98 percent chance that this device will be attacked?','No. It is a severity score under specified metric choices, not that probability.','Identify the version/vector and assess local applicability, exposure and consequences separately.'),
   check('A component name matches an advisory. Is affectedness fully established?','Not yet. Verify the relevant version/build, feature/configuration and advisory conditions.','Name matching can produce an uncertain finding; preserve its confidence and evidence limitations.'),
   check('Does internet reachability prove the service has already been compromised?','No. It describes an accessible path from that context, not an observed incident.','Exposure, exploitable weakness and evidence of exploitation are different claims.'),
   check('Does absence from a known-exploitation catalog make an applicable weakness harmless?','No. It is not proof that exploitation cannot occur.','Use threat information as one prioritisation input while considering the actual asset and consequence.')
  ],
  reflection:'Create priority rows for TRAIN-A and TRAIN-B. Write source severity separately from local urgency, name the uncertain facts, and explain a safe next step for each without sending requests to external services.',
  references:[ref('CVE: common vulnerability identifiers','https://www.cve.org/ResourcesSupport/FAQs'),ref('FIRST: CVSS version 4 specification','https://www.first.org/cvss/v4.0/specification-document'),ref('CISA: known-exploitation catalog','https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog')]
 },
 '6.3':{
  objective:'Explain the different boundaries protected by firewalls, segmentation and endpoint controls; interpret a fictional communication policy; and verify a control without disabling it.',
  sections:[
   h('1. List required communication before designing rules'),
   p('Week 2 introduced network roles, and Week 5 separated application permissions from browser controls. Now Maya’s fictional school service has a web frontend, an application component and a database. The browser needs the public lesson route; it does not need direct database administration access. Write the expected communication paths before discussing allow or deny rules.'),
   term('Firewall rule','A rule that permits or blocks traffic according to applicable conditions such as source, destination, protocol, port, application or connection state. Rule semantics, evaluation and defaults depend on the particular firewall or platform.'),
   term('Inbound, outbound and stateful filtering','Inbound/outbound describe traffic relative to the protected device or boundary. Stateful filtering tracks connection context so replies can be treated differently from new unsolicited connections. A direction label alone does not describe every rule or session condition.'),
   p('A firewall enabled label is one observation, not proof of the intended communication policy. Ask which profile/boundary the rule applies to, whether another rule changes the result, and which sources/destinations are represented. A useful permission is tied to the needed service and authorised source, rather than a blanket allow-anywhere shortcut.'),
   h('2. A fictional policy with clear purpose'),
   p('Paper policy: approved browsers may reach the public lesson service; only the application component may reach its database service; administration is limited to the designated management path; guest devices have no direct database route. This is a diagram/policy exercise, not a command set to apply to your home network. Real rules also need the platform’s evaluation semantics and operational dependencies such as approved update paths.'),
   p('Worked question: the browser cannot save a note, but it can load the public lesson. Do not disable all filtering to diagnose it. Identify the failed operation and dependencies. It could be an application permission, session, database or network-path issue. Use approved observations and a narrowly scoped check to isolate the boundary. A connectivity result does not itself establish application permission.'),
   h('3. Segmentation reduces unnecessary paths'),
   term('Network segmentation and enforcement','Segmentation separates communication into defined zones or paths. Its protective effect depends on enforced rules between them. Naming two networks separately, or assigning a VLAN, does not by itself establish that prohibited traffic is blocked.'),
   term('Lateral movement','Movement from one affected system or access context toward others. Limiting unnecessary paths and privileges can reduce some opportunities, but a zone label does not eliminate every movement route.'),
   p('Imagine a guest laptop is affected. A correctly enforced guest-to-database denial can reduce one route to the private database. The application still needs authentication, owner-note checks, parameter binding and safe output; segmentation cannot repair those requirements. Conversely, good application checks do not make unrestricted management access a suitable design.'),
   h('4. Endpoint controls protect the host layer'),
   term('Endpoint protection and EDR','Endpoint controls can include supported updates, host firewall policy, malware protection, execution restrictions and access rules. Endpoint detection and response, or EDR, collects/detects activity and may support response under an approved configuration. Features and coverage vary by product and deployment.'),
   p('Installing an agent is not the same as demonstrating coverage. Check whether it is active, updated, receiving the intended policy and reporting as expected. A clean scan does not prove every risk is absent. An unfamiliar process or alert needs investigation, not an unsupported diagnosis. Protective controls complement tested recovery because prevention and detection can both fail.'),
   p('A shared study device also depends on user/account context from Week 3. Ordinary work should not require broad administrative access, but a standard account can still read data it has permission to access. Choose controls for the particular boundary and keep their limitations visible; do not treat one enabled feature as complete safety.'),
   h('5. Verify allowed and denied paths'),
   term('Positive and negative control checks','A positive check verifies a required permitted operation works. A negative check verifies an operation the policy forbids is refused. Tests need an approved scope, expected outcome, observation context and a plan for any operational impact.'),
   p('Paper evidence rows: authorised browser to lesson service succeeds; guest to direct database route is refused; intended update path still works; private-note owner checks still apply. Record the test location and method. A screenshot of a rule is configuration evidence; an approved observed outcome is behaviour evidence. Both can be useful, but neither should be described as proving every path or every time.'),
   key('Firewalls filter communication, segmentation limits paths and endpoint controls cover host concerns. Verify the intended allowed/denied behaviours and keep application permissions and recovery as separate layers.'),
   tip('This lesson changes no firewall, router or endpoint setting. Begin with the fictional policy and explain the evidence you would ask an authorised administrator to obtain.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('A firewall is enabled. Does that establish the required database access policy?','No. Inspect the applicable rules and verify the relevant allowed/denied behaviour.','Enabled status alone does not establish who can reach which service under the active conditions.'),
   check('Two network zones have different names. Is isolation proven?','No. Protective separation needs effective rules and evidence for the relevant paths.','Labels and topology descriptions do not substitute for enforced access boundaries.'),
   check('A correctly restricted database path exists. May the app omit owner-note authorisation?','No. Network access and resource/action permission protect different requirements.','An allowed application connection can still request an unauthorised record operation.'),
   check('What should a control check include besides showing a deny?','A necessary legitimate operation still works, with scope and observation context recorded.','Security controls must preserve intended work while refusing prohibited actions.')
  ],
  reflection:'Draw the fictional browser, app, database and guest zones. Label required and prohibited paths, then propose two positive and two negative evidence rows. Explain what host protection still contributes and what it cannot establish.',
  references:[ref('Microsoft: host firewall roles and rules','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/windows-firewall/'),ref('NIST: controls in risk-management context','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework')]
 },
 '6.4':{
  objective:'Explain cloud shared responsibility across service models, connect identity and data settings to customer tasks, and write a small responsibility/evidence matrix without changing a real cloud account.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Cloud changes the work split, not the need for protection'),
   term('Cloud service and shared responsibility','A cloud service provides capabilities operated partly by a provider. Shared responsibility divides security tasks between provider and customer according to the chosen service, configuration and agreement. Shared does not mean nobody owns the task.'),
   p('Maya considers three fictional options: renting a virtual machine, using a managed application runtime, or using a hosted note service. These do not assign the same maintenance work. The provider’s protection of physical infrastructure does not automatically configure Maya’s accounts, sharing choices or application permissions appropriately. Read the particular service’s current documentation instead of guessing from the word cloud.'),
   h('2. Compare common service models carefully'),
   term('IaaS, PaaS and SaaS','Infrastructure as a Service commonly supplies virtualised infrastructure; Platform as a Service supplies a managed application platform; Software as a Service supplies a hosted application. These are broad learning categories, not complete responsibility contracts for every product.'),
   p('With a rented VM, the customer commonly manages its guest OS and installed application maintenance while the provider handles underlying infrastructure. With a managed platform, some runtime/OS work shifts to the provider, but application code, dependencies and configuration can remain customer tasks. With a hosted application, the provider operates more of the software, while customers still have account, permission, sharing and data-handling responsibilities. Exact boundaries vary; verify them for the actual service.'),
   p('A managed database is not automatically identical to a database you installed on a VM. Backup schedules, restore permissions, encryption/key choices and supported maintenance may differ. If a task is unclear, mark it unassigned/unknown and find the official responsibility rather than pretending a service category answers every detail.'),
   h('3. Identity and configuration remain important'),
   term('IAM, administrative account and service identity','Identity and access management, or IAM, controls identities and their permitted operations. An administrative account has powerful management access; a service identity represents an application/workload. Both need permissions suited to their tasks and protected access material.'),
   p('A learning application that reads one dataset should not receive account-wide administration simply because that permission makes setup easy. Protect privileged and recovery access; use appropriate strong authentication for human accounts and supported credential mechanisms for workloads. Week 4’s scope, session and revocation concepts still apply, although interfaces and options vary by provider.'),
   term('Resource policy and public access','A resource policy describes who can use a service resource. A public sharing setting can expose data outside the intended audience. Strong provider infrastructure does not correct a customer’s decision to publish a private archive.'),
   p('Worked case: a fictional storage folder contains public lesson images and private backup exports. Sharing both through one public configuration crosses their different data requirements. Separate the assets and access decisions. An encryption-at-rest setting does not make a publicly readable object private; the service may decrypt it for an allowed reader. Access control and encryption protect different paths.'),
   h('4. Availability, keys and recovery have owners too'),
   term('Customer-managed key and recovery dependency','Some services let a customer control a key or its access policy. That adds responsibility for continued key access and recovery. Losing or disabling required key material can make otherwise present encrypted data unusable.'),
   p('Do not assume provider redundancy is your historical backup or that an account can restore every earlier version. Determine retained copies, restore capability, keys, permissions and the provider/customer task split. Test recovery with invented data in an approved destination. The next day connects this to repository and backup decisions; it does not require buying a cloud subscription.'),
   p('Useful evidence can include a service responsibility document, a scoped role-policy review, a public-sharing test using approved sample objects, and a controlled restore result. A compliance logo or a general service availability claim does not establish that Maya’s particular folder is private or her particular application is recoverable.'),
   h('5. Build a responsibility matrix'),
   term('Responsibility matrix','A table naming a task, the responsible party, the service-specific source, verification evidence and any uncertainty. If provider and customer both contribute, describe each action rather than writing shared as an unexplained answer.'),
   p('Fictional rows: physical host maintenance—provider, supported by service documentation; guest OS updates on the rented VM—customer administrator; note-owner authorisation in a custom app—application team; user sharing choices—designated account owner; backup restore—named service/account owner with verified provider capabilities. For a managed platform, review the OS/runtime row again rather than copy the VM answer.'),
   key('Cloud responsibility depends on the selected service and task. Account protection, application rules, data sharing and tested recovery do not disappear just because infrastructure is managed.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Does provider-managed physical security establish that your storage object is private?','No. Its access policy and sharing configuration still need the correct customer decision and verification.','Infrastructure protection and resource audience are different responsibilities.'),
   check('Should you copy the rented-VM OS patching row unchanged to every managed platform?','No. Verify the actual service-specific responsibility split.','Common service-model categories help orient you but are not a complete task contract.'),
   check('Can encryption at rest replace a private resource policy?','No. An allowed public read may still return decrypted content through the service.','Encryption and reader permissions protect different access paths.'),
   check('What belongs in a responsibility matrix when the owner is unclear?','The uncertainty, a named follow-up and the documentation/evidence needed to resolve it.','Leaving a task ambiguously shared can leave necessary work undone.')
  ],
  reflection:'Compare a fictional VM, managed runtime and hosted note service. Write six task rows with owners and evidence, including account permissions, application controls and recovery. Mark at least one task requiring service-specific verification.',
  references:[ref('AWS: service-dependent shared responsibility','https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/'),ref('Microsoft: responsibility across cloud service models','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/security/fundamentals/shared-responsibility')]
 },
 '6.5':{
  objective:'Separate repository visibility from delivered-site content and secret protection; explain exposure response; and design backup verification around recovery objectives and dependencies.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Ask who can read each store'),
   term('Repository visibility and delivered website','Repository visibility controls who can access source through that repository’s access model. A delivered website serves its content to its allowed visitors. These are separate boundaries: information sent as HTML/JavaScript to a visitor can be inspected by that visitor.'),
   p('Maya’s fictional project has public teaching text, browser notes, a sync integration and a private backup export. They should not be treated as one store. A public source repository can intentionally distribute lessons while the user’s local browser notes remain in that browser context. Explicit sync/share, a shared profile or compromised access can change who reads those notes. Describe the actual data flow instead of assuming every note is public or every local value is a protected secret.'),
   term('Private, public and unlisted links','Public resources are available to their public audience. A genuinely private resource requires an applicable access check. An unlisted resource may merely be hard to discover; knowing its URL can still allow access. GitHub secret gists are unlisted rather than authenticated private stores.'),
   p('Knowing a secret gist’s identifier/link can allow reading its content. Treat that link and any personal stored content accordingly; the term secret in the interface is not the same as a private repository’s reader permission. This lesson does not inspect your real Gist, change your sync settings or move progress. It teaches the distinction so a future sharing decision can be made from accurate access assumptions.'),
   h('2. A filename is not a secret boundary'),
   p('Moving a token from public HTML into a shipped JavaScript file does not hide it. Base64, a hidden button or a masked field does not make publicly delivered data confidential. Private repositories also should not become ordinary credential vaults: authorised collaborators, integrations, clones and build processes may read them. Use an appropriate supported secret-management mechanism for a real deployment; a downloaded database file is not a private backend merely because it has a database extension.'),
   term('Exposure response and historical copies','If an access credential was exposed, revoke or rotate the affected access through the provider and review relevant activity. Latest-file cleanup does not invalidate previous copies, repository history or other retained artefacts.'),
   p('Worked incident: a fictional integration token appears in an old public commit but is missing from the newest file. First address the credential’s access lifecycle using the known provider route. Then plan supported source/history cleanup and review collaborators or automated outputs where appropriate. Changing repository visibility may reduce future access but cannot recall copies already obtained. Do not copy the value into a learning report or use it to test whether it works.'),
   h('3. Connect backups to the loss you must survive'),
   term('Recovery point objective (RPO)','The target amount of recent data loss you can tolerate, often expressed as time. It is a requirement for the plan, not proof that the current backup actually meets it.'),
   term('Recovery time objective (RTO)','The target time to restore the required useful service. It concerns getting the task working again, not merely finding a backup filename.'),
   p('Fictional target: the class can tolerate at most one day of lost notes and needs useful reading/writing restored within two hours. A daily backup schedule may support the first target if copies are successful and retained, but the latest successful restore point still needs checking. A three-day-old usable copy fails that one-day target even if the schedule says daily. Likewise, a decryptable archive that nobody can import within the needed time may fail the service recovery target.'),
   h('4. Verify the whole recovery dependency chain'),
   term('Restore-point evidence and dependency test','Evidence identifies which copy exists, what it contains, when it was captured and whether required access, keys, software and import steps work in a controlled destination. A checksum match supports byte agreement, not complete application recovery.'),
   p('Test using fictional notes in a separate destination/profile: obtain the intended copy, verify needed access/key availability, restore/import it, check the expected records, and perform a representative read/write operation. Record the capture time, loss window, elapsed recovery time and limitations. Keep real progress untouched during a learning exercise, especially where replacement import can overwrite current data.'),
   p('Separation matters: a copy on the same failed disk cannot survive that disk loss. Synchronising one deletion everywhere is not independent historical recovery. An offline or otherwise separately protected copy can reduce some shared failure paths, but only if it is maintained, accessible when needed and protected from unauthorised readers. A backup may contain private journals even when credentials were excluded.'),
   h('5. Write a publication and recovery policy'),
   p('Paper policy: publish authored lessons and fictional examples; keep personal exports and live credentials outside public source; record repository/integration readers; test recoverable sample data against stated RPO/RTO. For each control, identify evidence and a next review trigger. A provider’s general backup claim or a green export message cannot replace a usable recovery test of your specific data.'),
   key('Source visibility, delivered content, unlisted links and browser storage have different readers. Exposed access needs revocation/rotation, and backup success needs verified recovery against the task’s objectives.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Is a GitHub secret gist an authenticated private store merely because it is labelled secret?','No. It is unlisted; a person who knows the URL can read it.','Discovery visibility and access-controlled privacy are different boundaries.'),
   check('Will making a repository private invalidate a token already copied from a public commit?','No. Revoke or rotate the credential through the provider and review relevant activity.','Visibility changes cannot invalidate a previously obtained credential or recall its copies.'),
   check('The RPO target is one day, but the latest usable copy is three days old. Is the target demonstrated?','No. The usable recovery point exceeds the tolerated loss window.','An intended schedule is not evidence that current retained recovery points meet the requirement.'),
   check('A backup checksum matches. What remains to verify for useful recovery?','Required access/keys, actual import/restore, expected records and representative working functions within the recovery target.','Byte agreement alone does not show that the application can use the recovered data.')
  ],
  reflection:'Create a fictional publication-and-recovery plan with reader boundaries, secret-exposure response, RPO/RTO, capture evidence and a separate restore test. Explain one way sync or a required recovery key could affect the plan.',
  references:[ref('GitHub: repository visibility','https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/managing-repository-settings/setting-repository-visibility'),ref('GitHub: public and secret gist behaviour','https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/writing-on-github/editing-and-sharing-content-with-gists/creating-gists'),ref('GitHub: sensitive-data response and history','https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository'),ref('NCSC: recoverable backups','https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online/always-back-up-your-most-important-data')]
 },
 '6.6':{
  objective:'Write a finding supported by bounded evidence, propose remediation with ownership and verification, distinguish mitigation from a fix, and describe Python’s role in combining reports without losing uncertainty.',
  sections:[
   h('1. A finding must be useful to someone who acts on it'),
   term('Finding, evidence and requirement','A finding identifies a specific gap or concern relative to a requirement. Evidence is the observation supporting it. A useful report distinguishes the observed fact, its potential consequence and what remains uncertain.'),
   p('Poor report: the server is unsafe. Better fictional report: the approved inventory snapshot for APP-A records a build within the vendor’s affected range; the specific feature is confirmed enabled; the owner should evaluate the supported fix. The second statement supplies a scope and evidence rather than claiming every component is unsafe or asserting compromise without incident evidence.'),
   term('Asset identity and evidence provenance','Identify the actual system/resource and the source/time/context of an observation. A hostname, file name or duplicate display label alone may not uniquely identify an asset. Provenance lets another authorised person understand where the evidence came from.'),
   p('For this worksheet use training IDs, invented product names and relative observation times. Do not invent an actual CVE number, affected product advisory or live service finding. If a real approved report is later used, preserve the authoritative reference and current revision, record its permitted scope, and redact secrets/personal data from examples.'),
   h('2. Use a complete but small report template'),
   p('Template fields:\nFinding ID and title.\nAsset/resource and responsible owner.\nRequired baseline/policy/advisory condition.\nObserved state with source/time/context.\nApplicability and evidence confidence.\nPotential impact, reachability and existing controls.\nSource severity and local priority with reason.\nProposed action, dependencies and target/review date.\nVerification method and expected result.\nCurrent status, limitations and exception details if any.'),
   p('A screenshot can support a setting observation but may contain personal names or tokens. Use minimal redacted evidence with a permitted access location. Do not put a live token or raw private backup into the finding itself. When recording events, retain necessary task/decision information rather than indiscriminately exporting every available log. Week 7 will extend the investigation and incident-reporting side.'),
   h('3. Distinguish ways to address the concern'),
   term('Remediation, mitigation and accepted risk','Remediation addresses the underlying identified problem. Mitigation reduces a particular likelihood or consequence without necessarily removing the problem. Accepted risk is an accountable decision to tolerate a stated residual risk under defined conditions; it should not be an unexplained ignore flag.'),
   p('For fictional APP-A, applying the supported fix and verifying the affected condition is removed may remediate the issue. Restricting a public route can mitigate exposure while the change is planned. A documented temporary exception may accept remaining risk with an owner, reason, expiry/review and controls. These are different outcomes and should have different evidence. Removing one network path does not prove every exploitation prerequisite is impossible.'),
   term('Retest and closure criteria','A retest checks the specific requirement after the action. Closure criteria state what evidence is needed to mark the finding resolved. Important legitimate work should still function; a broken application is not automatically an acceptable security repair.'),
   h('4. Walk through a fictional finding'),
   p('TRAIN-01: approved snapshot shows a mismatch between the required host-firewall policy and the observed classroom device setting. Confidence: configuration mismatch is confirmed, reachability consequence not yet measured. Owner: designated lab administrator. Priority reason: the mismatch may open an unnecessary path, and the asset handles study records. Proposed action: owner reviews/restores the applicable policy through the supported change process. Verification: refreshed policy evidence plus approved checks showing the intended allowed and denied paths, and normal lesson/update functions remain available.'),
   p('Notice what this row does not say: it does not claim malware, does not assign a made-up CVSS score to a configuration gap, and does not instruct Maya to disable or replace firewall rules. If the observation is stale, mark that limitation and request current evidence. If the policy was legitimately changed, update the requirement/exception with the responsible approval rather than falsifying the observed row.'),
   h('5. Combine reports without erasing identity or uncertainty'),
   term('Finding correlation and deduplication','Related observations may describe the same issue, but deduplication needs an explicit identity rule such as asset plus weakness/condition plus relevant component. Keep observation sources and times; do not merge unrelated assets just because their titles look similar.'),
   p('Python can later combine approved inventory and vulnerability records, preserve source references, group repeated observations, and produce a priority report with missing fields flagged. It should not average severity scores across unrelated findings or convert unknown exploitability into confirmed safe. Keep the local reasoning and source severity separate; a script can organise evidence but cannot approve risk or grant testing permission.'),
   h('6. Make status truthful and reviewable'),
   p('Useful fictional statuses are needs evidence, confirmed/open, action planned, temporarily mitigated, pending verification, verified resolved, or approved exception with review conditions. Installed update belongs under pending verification until the relevant active state is checked. A failed or unavailable retest is not a pass. Record who owns the next step and what observation would change the status.'),
   key('A useful finding links a specific requirement, bounded evidence, local consequence, accountable action and observable closure. Preserve uncertainty and verify the requirement after the change.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('An update was installed, but the affected active state has not been checked. Which status is appropriate?','Pending verification, with the required retest and owner recorded.','The action and evidence of successful remediation are separate.'),
   check('A route was restricted while the vulnerable component remains. Is underlying remediation established?','No. That can be a mitigation of the stated route, with remaining risk and follow-up documented.','Reduced exposure does not by itself prove the weakness was removed.'),
   check('Two findings have the same title on different assets. Should they automatically become one row?','No. Apply an explicit identity/correlation rule and preserve asset/source distinctions.','A shared label is not proof that the observations concern one resource or one issue.'),
   check('What should a closure check establish besides disappearance of a warning?','The specific required condition is satisfied and important legitimate functions remain usable, with evidence recorded.','A missing warning, unavailable scan or broken service is not adequate proof of a successful repair.')
  ],
  reflection:'Write TRAIN-01 using the report template. Add one temporary mitigation and its expiry/review condition, then define a retest and truthful status. Describe how a future Python report should retain asset identity, provenance and unknowns.',
  references:[ref('NIST: risk, roles and evidence outcomes','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework'),ref('NIST: verifying maintenance actions','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/40/r4/final'),ref('OWASP: proportionate protected event evidence','https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html')]
 },
 '6.7':{
  objective:'Connect maintenance, exposure, control boundaries, cloud responsibilities and recovery in one fictional case, then produce reasoned findings and verification criteria from closed-notes recall.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Recall the purpose of each control'),
   p('Use today for retrieval and rest. Before opening earlier notes, define baseline, drift, inventory, vulnerability, CVE, CVSS, exposure, mitigation, shared responsibility, RPO, RTO and retest. For each term give one fictional observation and one limitation. Remembering a label is useful, but the learning goal is to explain what decision the evidence supports.'),
   term('Evidence-led weekly review','A review that distinguishes requirement, observation, uncertainty, action and verification. It asks what would change your conclusion rather than treating a checklist tick as proof of complete safety.'),
   h('2. Read the integrated case carefully'),
   p('Fictional case: Maya’s learning project uses a supported laptop and a rented cloud VM. An inventory report shows a reader update downloaded but a restart pending. A vulnerability report mentions an old VM component, but its exact active build is unknown. A private-backup object is unintentionally shared publicly. A made-up integration token remains in an old public commit. The last usable note backup is three days old; the written RPO is one day. A network-zone diagram exists, but no approved behaviour check has verified the database path rules.'),
   p('Separate the concerns. Pending activation is maintenance verification. Unknown active build is vulnerability applicability. Public backup access is a confirmed sharing-policy problem, not something encryption at rest automatically fixes. Token exposure needs an access lifecycle response. Backup freshness fails the stated recovery target. A segmentation drawing is not behavioural evidence. None of these observations alone proves an actual compromise of every system.'),
   h('3. Choose next actions without skipping ownership'),
   p('A model approach is to engage the designated owners through their known routes: revoke/rotate the exposed integration credential and review relevant activity; contain unintended backup sharing while preserving necessary evidence; verify required update activation and current component data; assess applicability/priority using authoritative guidance and actual exposure; correct/test recovery against the agreed objectives; and plan approved allowed/denied path checks. If incident evidence appears, follow the incident process rather than treating it as just routine maintenance.'),
   p('Actions can run in parallel when independent, but dependencies matter. A cloud maintenance step needs the service’s responsibility split. A restore needs required access, keys and a separate destination. History cleanup needs collaboration planning. A restricted route can be a temporary mitigation, not an automatic closed finding. Write who acts, what is expected and what evidence is still missing.'),
   h('4. Build four small deliverables'),
   p('Deliverable1: a role-specific baseline/inventory comparison with match, mismatch and unknown. Deliverable2: two vulnerability/configuration findings with source severity separate from reasoned local priority. Deliverable3: a firewall/segmentation and cloud responsibility matrix stating permitted tasks and evidence. Deliverable4: a publication/recovery plan with reader boundaries, RPO/RTO and a tested restore dependency chain. All records use fictional identifiers; no real secret belongs in them.'),
   term('Review trigger and residual risk','A review trigger is an event or date that requires reconsidering a decision, such as a new advisory, changed exposure or expiring exception. Residual risk is what remains after the stated controls/actions, rather than an implied zero risk.'),
   p('For each deliverable, add one review trigger and one residual uncertainty. Example: temporary database-path restriction reviewed when the supported component update is ready; the absence of a current allowed/denied test remains a limitation. Another example: a backup schedule reviewed after any failed restore or loss of key access. Requirements should remain connected to real work and responsible owners.'),
   h('5. Practise explaining the evidence to another person'),
   p('Attempt the twelve checks below without notes, then reveal one answer at a time. Explain why an alternative would be wrong: CVSS as probability, unknown build as safe, private repository as a credential vault, a VLAN label as complete isolation, installed update as verified fixed, or a matching digest as fully usable recovery. Do not merely memorise the model sentence; connect it to the requirement and observation.'),
   p('Revisit Day1 for maintenance and baseline evidence, Day2 for applicability and priority, Day3 for layered controls, Day4 for service-specific responsibility, Day5 for publication/recovery and Day6 for finding/retest quality. Python’s role is still organising approved comparison/report records; it does not decide risk acceptance or justify probing someone else’s system. Next week expands how useful observations become monitoring and incident-response work.'),
   key('Maintain the system, limit unnecessary paths, protect reader boundaries and verify recovery. A defensible finding states what is known, what remains unknown, who acts and what demonstrates success.'),
   tip('Finish the fictional deliverables and note two unclear concepts with the day to revisit. Rest after retrieval rather than adding unplanned tools or changes to your everyday devices.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('A baseline differs from an observed setting. What is established first?','A discrepancy, with context and cause still to investigate.','Drift is not automatically evidence of malicious action.'),
   check('A required update downloaded but activation/restart is pending. May the finding be verified resolved?','No. Verify the relevant active state and required functions after the authorised maintenance.','The maintenance action and its effective result are different observations.'),
   check('Does a CVE identifier supply every deployment’s risk and complete fix plan?','No. Check applicable advisory conditions, current asset evidence and local consequence.','Identification is not a substitute for applicability or remediation planning.'),
   check('Is a CVSS score a direct probability of compromise?','No. It describes severity under recorded metrics; local risk needs context.','Severity, likelihood, applicability and actual incident evidence are separate concepts.'),
   check('An affected component is not internet-facing. Is risk automatically zero?','No. Other access paths, authorised misuse, dependencies and impact still matter.','A restricted route can reduce exposure without eliminating the underlying concern.'),
   check('A segmentation diagram exists. What evidence is still needed?','Applicable enforced rules and approved observations of required allowed/denied paths.','Separate labels or a drawing do not prove protective behaviour.'),
   check('Can endpoint protection replace application owner-note authorisation?','No. Host protection and resource/action checks cover different requirements.','Layers complement one another rather than making other boundaries unnecessary.'),
   check('Who owns guest OS maintenance in every cloud service?','It depends on the specific service/task; verify the documented responsibility.','A rented VM and a managed platform can divide work differently.'),
   check('Does a secret gist have the same authenticated private-reader boundary as a private repository?','No. A secret gist is unlisted and can be read by someone who knows its URL.','Discoverability and access-controlled privacy are different properties.'),
   check('What must happen to a token already exposed in repository history?','Revoke or rotate affected access and review activity, then plan supported source/history cleanup.','Visibility changes or a newest-file deletion cannot invalidate retained copies.'),
   check('A one-day RPO is written, but the usable copy is three days old. What follows?','The current recovery-point evidence does not meet the target; repair and verify the recovery plan.','A target or schedule does not prove actual recoverability/freshness.'),
   check('A finding is temporarily mitigated. What belongs in its continuing record?','Residual concern, owner, control scope, review/expiry condition and required underlying remediation/retest.','Temporary risk reduction is not automatically a permanent verified fix.')
  ],
  reflection:'Complete the integrated case’s four deliverables. For each finding, write one evidence gap, one accountable next action and one closure test. Identify your two least clear concepts without recording real account or credential details.',
  references:[ref('NIST: cybersecurity framework outcomes','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework'),ref('FIRST: severity metric context','https://www.first.org/cvss/v4.0/specification-document'),ref('GitHub: sensitive-data response','https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository'),ref('NCSC: backup and restore planning','https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online/always-back-up-your-most-important-data')]
 }
};
